          "transcript": [
            "Conversation 1.",
            "M: Welcome to the Book Club.",
            "M: Today's guest is Susan Lane, the author of a new book on personal finances that has already sold half a million copies.",
            "M: Hi, Susan.",
            "M: Your book is extremely successful.",
            "M: Why do you think that is?",
            "F: I think that's because of my message, which is making happiness a priority over money.",
            "F: So many of us in my generation have spent decades trying to earn more money just to consume more, but it made us more miserable.",
            "M: You yourself were once caught in that cycle, working for two decades as an executive and earning a high salary, but still accumulating debt.",
            "F: I most certainly was.",
            "F: I earned millions, but by the time I quit my job four years ago to become a writer, I owed over $30,000.",
            "M: So how did you escape that pattern, and what would you advise other people to do?",
            "F: The first change is in what we value.",
            "F: We need to emphasize things that actually make us happy, like relationships, the environment, or even our hobbies.",
            "F: Once we make the right things our priority, our goals will change, and so will our financial behavior.",
            "M: How does that translate into practical action?",
            "M: Can you give our audience examples of what you're talking about on an everyday basis?",
            "F: The major areas for action are usually housing, food, and transportation.",
            "F: So, people might share a home with friends instead of living on their own, bring lunch from home instead of going to restaurants, and use public transport instead of owning a car.",
            "M: Those sound like major sacrifices.",
            "M: I could never share my home.",
            "M: I need my own space.",
            "F: But they aren't sacrifices.",
            "F: When people change their values, their desires change.",
            "F: So in the example of housing, if we value relationships, sharing a home isn't depriving ourselves of space, but giving us an opportunity to spend more time with our loved ones.",
            "M: Indeed it is."
          ],
