          "transcript": [
            "Conversation 2.",
            "F: Today on BookTalk, we're lucky enough to host John Robbins and discuss his new book, Why Americans Are Fat and How We Can Lose Weight.",
            "F: John isn't just a respected writer, he's also one of the rare celebrity authors writing about science today.",
            "M: Thanks for having me, Rebecca, but I'm hardly a celebrity.",
            "F: That's very modest of you to say, considering that your four books have sold a total of 7 million copies worldwide, and they've been translated into 12 different languages.",
            "F: What makes people so fascinated with your work?",
            "M: Well, people read my books because more than 60% of Americans are overweight or obese, and other countries are facing similar problems.",
            "M: Basically, we all want to know how to fix things.",
            "F: We certainly do.",
            "F: I've read your new book, and it's fabulous, especially when it comes to the way you make difficult science easy for laymen to understand.",
            "F: That's no small achievement.",
            "M: I'm glad to hear you find my work accessible, because I was worried when I wrote it that discussing the science might make the book more suited for a specialist audience.",
            "M: My last book was written primarily for the medical community, but this time, I want to help ordinary people take control of their weight.",
            "F: And how do you suggest they do that?",
            "F: Can you give us the basics of your advice for people who want to lose weight?",
            "M: Briefly, I argue that every person needs to consider their metabolism and eat what suits their body's needs.",
            "M: I don't advocate one single diet.",
            "M: Some people should eat more carbohydrates than others, and different people need different amounts of protein and fat.",
            "F: But you do have some recommendations for everyone, including eating 10 servings of vegetables and 3 of fruit a day.",
            "F: We'll talk about those recommendations next, but now we need to take a short break for a message from our sponsor."
          ],
