          "transcript": [
            "Conversation 1.",
            "M: How are you enjoying your new job?",
            "F: So far, so good.",
            "F: I don't miss having managers who deliver blunt, harsh feedback in the name of efficiency.",
            "M: From the way you described your last company, no wonder they had a problem with high staff turnover.",
            "F: Yeah, I couldn't wait to get out of there once my contract expired.",
            "F: The problem with a company culture that prizes directness above all else is that it creates a toxic culture of brilliant jerks that drives people out and erodes itself from within.",
            "M: My company's managers tend to be accommodating and kind, overlooking mistakes or issues so as not to hurt feelings.",
            "M: Issues often get ignored there until they build up and reach a crisis point.",
            "F: That's not surprising.",
            "F: My new company seems to employ a feedback policy that combines compassion and directness.",
            "F: Employees have the power to speak up, give feedback, disagree, and discuss problems in real time.",
            "F: It seems to help us to course correct, improve, and meet challenges, while also building teams that collaborate and care for one another.",
            "M: But that would be based on an atmosphere of mutual trust, wouldn't it?",
            "M: Otherwise people might interpret feedback as some kind of personal attack.",
            "F: True.",
            "F: Without an atmosphere of trust, feedback can create stress and self-doubt.",
            "F: But I think when we get feedback from someone we trust, we understand that the feedback isn't some kind of personal attack.",
            "F: It's actually a kind of support, because it's offered in the spirit of helping us improve.",
            "F: I think sometimes people need to shift their mindsets around how they receive feedback.",
            "M: Yes.",
            "M: Constructive feedback, after all, is how we learn and grow.",
            "M: It's the basis for healthy parenting, lasting friendships, career development, and so much more.",
            "M: If we shelter our children, friends, and colleagues from information that might enrich and enhance their lives, we're not being caring.",
            "M: We're actually doing harm to them.",
            "F: That's exactly right."
          ],
