          "transcript": [
            "Conversation 1.",
            "M: Welcome to Workplace!",
            "M: And in today's program, we're looking at the results of two recently published surveys, which both deal with the same topic, happiness at work.",
            "M: John, tell us about the first survey.",
            "F: Well, this was done by a human resources consultancy who interviewed more than 1,000 workers and established a top 10 of the factors which make people happy at work.",
            "F: The most important factor for the majority of the people interviewed was having friendly, supportive colleagues.",
            "F: In fact, 73% of people interviewed put their relationship with colleagues as the key factor contributing to happiness at work, which is a very high percentage.",
            "F: The second most important factor was having work that is enjoyable.",
            "F: The two least important factors were having one's achievements recognised and, rather surprisingly, earning a competitive salary.",
            "M: So, we're not mainly motivated by money?",
            "F: Apparently not.",
            "M: Any other interesting information in the survey?",
            "F: Yes. For example, 25% of the working people interviewed described themselves as very happy at work.",
            "F: However, 20% of employees described themselves as being unhappy.",
            "M: That's quite a lot of unhappy people at work every day.",
            "F: It is, isn't it?",
            "F: And there were several more interesting conclusions revealed by the survey.",
            "F: First of all, small is beautiful.",
            "F: People definitely prefer working for smaller organisations or companies with less than 100 staff.",
            "F: We also find out that, generally speaking, women were happier in their work than men.",
            "F: Yes, we are, aren't we?",
            "F: And workers on part-time contracts, who only work 4 or 5 hours a day, are happier than those who work full-time.",
            "F: The researchers concluded that this is probably due to a better work-life balance.",
            "M: Are bosses happier than their employees?",
            "F: Yes, perhaps not surprisingly.",
            "F: The higher people go in a company, the happier they are.",
            "F: So senior managers enjoy their jobs more than people working under them."
          ],
