          "transcript": [
            "Conversation 2.",
            "F: Mr. DeKaiser, I'm a great lover of your book, Moments Before the Flood. Can you tell us how you first became interested in this subject matter?",
            "M: In 2006, when the concert hall of the city of Bruges asked me to take some pictures for a catalogue for a new concert season around the theme of water, I found myself working along the Belgian coastline.",
            "M: As there had been numerous alarming articles in the press about a climate catastrophe waiting to happen, I started looking at the sea and the beach very differently, a place where I spent so many perfect days as a child.",
            "M: This fear of a looming danger became the subject of a large-scale photo project.",
            "M: It is clear now that it's a matter of time before the entire European coastline disappears under the water.",
            "M: The same goes for numerous big cities around the world.",
            "M: My idea was to photograph this beautiful and very unique coastline, rich in history, before it's too late. As a last witness.",
            "F: Can you talk a bit about how history plays a role in this project?",
            "M: Sure, the project is also about the history of Europe looking at the sea and wondering when the next enemy would appear.",
            "M: In the images, you see all kinds of possible defense constructions to hold back the Romans, Germans, Vikings, and now nature as enemy number one.",
            "M: For example, there is the image of the bridge into the sea, taken at the Normandy D-Day landing site.",
            "M: Also Venice, the city eternally threatened by the sea, where every morning wooden pathways have to be set up to allow tourists to reach their hotels.",
            "F: Thank you Mr. DeKaiser. It was a pleasure to have you with us today."
          ],
